  "text": "Researchers familiar with the ecological approach to mental illness have long examined how rates of psychiatric conditions vary across neighborhoods. Early work compared data from a major Midwestern metropolis with a smaller coastal city to see if earlier findings held elsewhere. Subsequent investigations looked at patients admitted to a state psychiatric facility in another region, and a collaborative project gathered information from six additional urban centers in the Midwest. While results from the first three locations have been published, data from the remaining cities are still being processed, so the overall picture remains incomplete. These ecological studies generally map the spatial distribution of social groups, cultural traits, and demographic factors, assuming that a neighborhood’s location and its residents’ social status are linked and that changes in one reflect changes in the other through ongoing competitive and migratory processes."
